    "name": "bodh-funnel-metrics",
    "text": "---\nname: bodh-funnel-metrics\ndescription: What BODH's funnel numbers mean and how to judge a channel.\n---\n\n# Bodhlok funnel metrics\n\nThe funnel: **click → registration → activation → paid**.\n- Registration: a free Bodhlok desk created.\n- Activation: the user opened their first chart in the desk (\"bring your first chart to the desk\"). This is the north-star metric for marketing, because people who work with a real chart are the ones who upgrade.\n- Paid: first purchase (a paid plan, institute seats, or AI consult credits).\n\nAttribution is first touch: each user is credited to the channel and campaign of their first tracked visit (UTMs, ad click ids). Organic social without UTMs shows up as Direct or Organic Social, so organic numbers are undercounted.\n\n**Judge channels by cost per activated user (CPA, in ₹)**, then activation rate (activations / registrations) and paid rate. Clicks and registrations alone are vanity. Compare with the previous period of the same length, not with one good day. A channel needs at least about 30 activations in the period before you call it better or worse than another; below that, say the data is thin.\n\n**Good signs**: CPA falling while volume holds; activation rate above about 40%. **Warning signs**: registrations up but activation rate down (low-intent traffic or a broken onboarding step); spend concentrated in one ad set.\n\nSpend changes, bids and budgets are not yours to make. You recommend; people approve in the app, and the guardrails enforce caps.\n\nWhen the numbers are missing (no tracking data yet), say so plainly and base the plan on content and search data instead. Never invent numbers.\n"
